      "message": "Thanks for the updates and my apologies for the delay. I decided to use gemini to help me review this CL. This is the first time I am doing this, so I hope you are okay being my guinea pig.\n\nInstead of leaving dozens of inline comments, I\u0027m consolidating my feedback here because most of the points tie back to unifying and simplifying the implementation.\n\n#### 1. Unifying the 2-argument and \u003e2-argument algorithms\n\n  Right now, AccurateNorm(a, b) and AccurateNorm(a, b, args...) still take two very different paths:\n\n  • Scaling: The 2-argument overload only scales when inputs are near overflow/underflow, whereas the ≥ 3-argument overload unconditionally calls std::ilogb and N + 1 calls to std::scalbn on every\n  invocation (even when all inputs are 1.0, which is the common case in rotation.h).\n  • Preprocessing: The 2-argument overload uses simple fabs/isinf/isnan checks, whereas the variadic overload constructs two std::initializer_lists (values and magnitudes), calls std::find_if twice,\n  calls std::accumulate with std::fmax, sorts only the first two normalized arguments via std::minmax (which is no longer needed with TwoSum), and passes the remaining args... to scalbn without fabs.\n  • Accumulation: UnscaledAccurateSquareNormWithError(x, y) and AccumulateSquareNormWithError(sigma, sigma_e, x, args...) duplicate the same step.\n\n  How to unify them:\n\n  1. Single UnscaledAccurateNorm for all N ≥ 2:\n  \n  Starting from sigma \u003d x * x and sigma_e \u003d fma(x, x, -sigma), a single fold expression over the remaining arguments handles both N \u003d 2 and N \u003e 2:\n    template \u003ctypename T, typename... Args\u003e\n    constexpr T UnscaledAccurateNorm(T x, Args... args) {\n      using std::fma;\n      using std::sqrt;\n\n      T sigma \u003d x * x;\n      T sigma_e \u003d fma(x, x, -sigma);\n      auto accumulate \u003d [\u0026sigma, \u0026sigma_e](T v) {\n        const T v_sq \u003d v * v;\n        const auto [new_sigma, sum_e] \u003d TwoSum(sigma, v_sq);\n        sigma \u003d new_sigma;\n        sigma_e +\u003d sum_e + fma(v, v, -v_sq);\n      };\n      (accumulate(args), ...);\n\n      const T h \u003d sqrt(sigma);\n      const T tau \u003d sigma_e + fma(-h, h, sigma);\n      return fma(tau / h, T(0.5), h);\n    }\n\n  2. Conditional scaling based on maximum for all N ≥ 2:\n  \n  Because radix-2 scaling only shifts exponents without changing mantissa bits, UnscaledAccurateNorm is invariant to scaling whenever no intermediate value overflows or underflows:\n      • Overflow cannot occur if maximum ≤ √(F_{max}/N).\n      • Any argument that can affect the double-word sum (σ, σₑ) satisfies |aᵢ| ≥ ε · maximum. And fma(a_i, a_i, -a_i^2) is normal (≥ F_{min}) whenever |aᵢ| ≥ 2^{⌈(e_{min}-1+p)/2⌉} (2^-484 for double).\n      Thus, when maximum ≥ 2^{⌈(e_{min}-1+3p)/2⌉} (2^-431 for double, 2^-27 for float), no significant argument can underflow its square or FMA residual.\n      • This means for any N ≥ 2, if maximum is in the safe normal range, we can call UnscaledAccurateNorm directly with zero ilogb/scalbn calls; and only when maximum is outside that range do we\n      scale by exponent \u003d ilogb(maximum).\n\n \n#### 2. Numerical bugs\n\n  1. KahanBabuskaNeumaierSum (compensated_math.h:58) is incorrect and unnecessary:\n    for (T value : values) {\n      std::tie(sum, error) \u003d TwoSum(sum, value + error);\n    }\n  Adding value + error before TwoSum is not error-free: whenever |value| \u003e\u003e |error|, value + error rounds off error, and std::tie(sum, error) \u003d ... overwrites error. For example,\n  KahanBabuskaNeumaierSum({1.0, 1e-16, -1.0}) returns {0.0, 0.0} (losing 1e-16 completely), whereas [s, e] \u003d TwoSum(sum, value); sum \u003d s; error +\u003d e; returns {0.0, 1e-16}.\n  More importantly, KahanBabuskaNeumaierSum is not needed here at all: sigma_e, sum_e, and x_error are already O(εσ) second-order compensation terms that get collapsed into a single scalar T at every\n  step anyway. Plain addition sigma_e +\u003d sum_e + x_error (as in Borges Algorithm 1014) takes 2 additions instead of 22 and gives identical accuracy.\n\n  2. AccurateNormTraits\u003cT\u003e::CutoffRatio() (accurate_norm.h:97) is off by √2 when digits is even (float):\n    return scalbn(constants::sqrt_2_v\u003cT\u003e / T{2},\n    \t\t  (1 - std::numeric_limits\u003cT\u003e::digits) / 2);\n  The rewrite √(ε/2) \u003d 2^{-p/2} \u003d (√2/2) · 2^{(1-p)/2} assumes 1 - p is even. For float, digits \u003d 24 (even), so (1 - 24) / 2 truncates -23 / 2 to -11, and multiplying by √2/2 produces 2^{-11.5} \u003d √ε\n  (0x1.6a09e6p-12) instead of √(ε/2) \u003d 2⁻¹² (0x1p-12).\n      • Reproducer: For float x \u003d 1.5f (0x1.8p+0) and float y \u003d x * CutoffRatio() (0x1.0f876cp-11), AccurateNorm(x, y) hits y \u003c\u003d x * CutoffRatio() and returns 0x1.8p+0 (a 0.75 ULP error), whereas the\n      exact float norm (and AccurateNorm(x, y, 0.0f)) is 0x1.800002p+0.\n\n  3. AccurateNormTraits\u003cT\u003e::Tiny() (accurate_norm.h:111) is too small to prevent FMA underflow:\n  Tiny() \u003d 2^-511 (for double) only keeps y * y \u003e\u003d F_min (2^-1022), but fma(x, x, -x_sq), fma(y, y, -y_sq), and fma(-h, h, sigma) are smaller by up to 2⁻⁵³ and underflow to subnormal/zero when x, y ∈\n  [2⁻⁵¹¹, 2⁻⁴⁸⁵].\n      • Reproducer: For x \u003d 0x1.4a15545f86ef4p-510 and y \u003d 0x1.3a578a98bcf9dp-510, AccurateNorm(x, y) does not scale (y \u003e\u003d Tiny()), underflows the FMA corrections, and returns 0x1.c7d03b992e6a9p-510\n      (1 ULP off), disagreeing with AccurateNorm(x, y, 0.0) which returns the correctly rounded 0x1.c7d03b992e6a8p-510.\n\n\n#### 3. Performance / Codegen issues\n\n  • AccurateNormTraits methods call std::scalbn at runtime: In C++17, std::scalbn is not constexpr. At -O3 on Clang, AccurateNorm(double, double) emits three calls to _scalbn on the unscaled fast path\n  just to compute CutoffRatio(), Huge(), and Tiny(). These thresholds should be static constexpr constants.\n  • Pass scalar Args... by value instead of Args\u0026\u0026...: Taking scalar Args\u0026\u0026... by forwarding reference causes std::forward\u003cArgs\u003e(args) to be expanded three times on the same pack (accurate_norm.h:291,\n  317, 344) and causes -O3 codegen to spill lvalue double arguments to the stack.\n  • jet.h (AccurateNorm):\n      • Constrain Args to Jet\u003cT, N\u003e and take const Args\u0026... args.\n      • Use a binary left fold ((x.a / tmp * x.v + y.a / tmp * y.v) + ... + (args.a / tmp * args.v)) to avoid the derivative lambda and the extra + Eigen::Vector\u003cT, N\u003e::Zero() addition.\n  • rotation.h (AnyExactlyNonZero):\n      • Take const T\u0026... values by const reference instead of by value so Jet\u003cdouble, N\u003e objects are not copied.\n      • In AngleAxisToRotationMatrix, RotationMatrixToEulerAngles, and AngleAxisRotatePoint, AccurateNorm is called before if (internal::AnyExactlyNonZero(theta)) (unlike AngleAxisToQuaternion and\n      QuaternionToAngleAxis where AnyExactlyNonZero(a0, a1, a2) is checked before AccurateNorm). Calling AccurateNorm on zero Jets evaluates 0.0 / 0.0 \u003d NaN in theta.v before the branch.\n\n\n#### 4. Style, conventions, and tests (accurate_norm_test.cc)\n\n  • Exceptions: UlpDistance throws std::domain_error (accurate_norm_test.cc:128, 135), which violates the Google C++ Style Guide / Ceres conventions.\n  • Simplify UlpDistance / UlpDistance2:\n      • accurate_norm_test.cc checks #if defined(CERES_HAS_CPP20) on line 44 without #include \"ceres/internal/port.h\", so CERES_HAS_CPP20 is never defined there.\n      • UlpDistance2 is never used by MaxNumUlp, and over 200 lines of accurate_norm_test.cc are devoted to MakeInteger, UlpDistance2, and UlpDistance. Since every MaxNumUlp check in the test uses a\n      tolerance of 0 or 1 ULP, a short loop using std::nextafter(a, b) can replace both functions portably across all floating-point types.\n  • Missing Jet tests: The commit message highlights Jet derivatives for variadic AccurateNorm as the main motivation, but there are no tests calling AccurateNorm on Jets (e.g., verifying 2-, 3-, and\n  4-argument Jet derivatives, including when a subset of components are zero).\n  • Minor cleanups:\n      • Remove unused kExpectedRNorm in accurate_norm_test.cc:439.\n      • Fix header guard in accurate_norm.h (CERES_PUBLIC_ACCURATE_NORM_H_ instead of CERES_PUBLIC_ACCURATE_NORM_) and remove the stale comments about the reciprocal variant (AccurateRNorm).",
